}}},"48153":{"id":48153,"crate_id":0,"name":null,"span":{"filename":"library\\core\\src\\..\\..\\portable-simd\\crates\\core_simd\\src\\simd\\num\\uint.rs","begin":[262,1],"end":[262,72]},"visibility":"default","docs":null,"links":{},"attrs":[],"deprecation":null,"inner":{"impl":{"is_unsafe":false,"generics":{"params":[{"name":"N","kind":{"const":{"type":{"primitive":"usize"},"default":null}}}],"where_predicates":[{"bound_predicate":{"type":{"resolved_path":{"path":"LaneCount","id":9493,"args":{"angle_bracketed":{"args":[{"const":{"expr":"N","value":null,"is_literal":false}}],"constraints":[]}}}},"bounds":[{"trait_bound":{"trait":{"path":"SupportedLaneCount","id":9494,"args":null},"generic_params":[],"modifier":"none"}}],"generic_params":[]}}]},"provided_trait_methods":[],"trait":{"path":"SimdUint","id":47274,"args":null},"for":{"resolved_path":{"path":"Simd","id":9499,"args":{"angle_bracketed":{"args":[{"type":{"primitive":"usize"}},{"const":{"expr":"N","value":null,"is_literal":false}}],"constraints":[]}}}},"items":[48131,48132,48133,48134,48135,48136,48137,48138,48139,48140,48141,48142,48143,48144,48145,48146,48147,48148,48149,48150,48151,48152],"is_negative":false,"is_synthetic":false,"blanket_impl":null}}},"8408":{"id":8408,"crate_id":0,"name":"offset_from","span":{"filename":"library\\core\\src\\ptr\\const_ptr.rs","begin":[612,5],"end":[620,6]},"visibility":"public","docs":"Calculates the distance between two pointers within the same allocation. The returned value is in\nunits of T: the distance in bytes divided by `size_of::<T>()`.\n\nThis is equivalent to `(self as isize - origin as isize) / (size_of::<T>() as isize)`,\nexcept that it has a lot more opportunities for UB, in exchange for the compiler\nbetter understanding what you are doing.\n\nThe primary motivation of this method is for computing the `len` of an array/slice\nof `T` that you are currently representing as a \"start\" and \"end\" pointer\n(and \"end\" is \"one past the end\" of the array).\nIn that case, `end.offset_from(start)` gets you the length of the array.\n\nAll of the following safety requirements are trivially satisfied for this usecase.\n\n[`offset`]: #method.offset\n\n# Safety\n\nIf any of the following conditions are violated, the result is Undefined Behavior:\n\n* `self` and `origin` must either\n\n  * point to the same address, or\n  * both be [derived from][crate::ptr#provenance] a pointer to the same [allocation], and the memory range between\n    the two pointers must be in bounds of that object. (See below for an example.)\n\n* The distance between the pointers, in bytes, must be an exact multiple\n  of the size of `T`.\n\nAs a consequence, the absolute distance between the pointers, in bytes, computed on\nmathematical integers (without \"wrapping around\"), cannot overflow an `isize`. This is\nimplied by the in-bounds requirement, and the fact that no allocation can be larger\nthan `isize::MAX` bytes.\n\nThe requirement for pointers to be derived from the same allocation is primarily\nneeded for `const`-compatibility: the distance between pointers into *different* allocated\nobjects is not known at compile-time. However, the requirement also exists at\nruntime and may be exploited by optimizations. If you wish to compute the difference between\npointers that are not guaranteed to be from the same allocation, use `(self as isize -\norigin as isize) / size_of::<T>()`.\n\n[`add`]: #method.add\n[allocation]: crate::ptr#allocation\n\n# Panics\n\nThis function panics if `T` is a Zero-Sized Type (\"ZST\").\n\n# Examples\n\nBasic usage:\n\n```\nlet a = [0; 5];\nlet ptr1: *const i32 = &a[1];\nlet ptr2: *const i32 = &a[3];\nunsafe {\n    assert_eq!(ptr2.offset_from(ptr1), 2);\n    assert_eq!(ptr1.offset_from(ptr2), -2);\n    assert_eq!(ptr1.offset(2), ptr2);\n    assert_eq!(ptr2.offset(-2), ptr1);\n}\n```\n\n*Incorrect* usage:\n\n```rust,no_run\nlet ptr1 = Box::into_raw(Box::new(0u8)) as *const u8;\nlet ptr2 = Box::into_raw(Box::new(1u8)) as *const u8;\nlet diff = (ptr2 as isize).wrapping_sub(ptr1 as isize);\n// Make ptr2_other an \"alias\" of ptr2.add(1), but derived from ptr1.\nlet ptr2_other = (ptr1 as *const u8).wrapping_offset(diff).wrapping_offset(1);\nassert_eq!(ptr2 as usize, ptr2_other as usize);\n// Since ptr2_other and ptr2 are derived from pointers to different objects,\n// computing their offset is undefined behavior, even though\n// they point to addresses that are in-bounds of the same object!\nunsafe {\n    let one = ptr2_other.offset_from(ptr2); // Undefined Behavior! ⚠️\n}\n```","links":{"crate::ptr#provenance":7868,"crate::ptr#allocation":7868},"attrs":[{"other":"#[<cfg_attr_trace>(miri, track_caller)]"},{"other":"#[attr = ConstStability {stability: PartialConstStability {level: Stable {since: Version(RustcVersion {major: 1,\nminor: 65, patch: 0})}, feature: \"const_ptr_offset_from\", promotable: false}}]"},{"other":"#[attr = Stability {stability: Stability {level: Stable {since: Version(RustcVersion {major: 1,\nminor: 47, patch: 0})}, feature: \"ptr_offset_from\"}}]"},{"other":"#[attr = Inline(Hint)]"}],"deprecation":null,"inner":{"function":{"sig":{"inputs":[["self",{"generic":"Self"}],["origin",{"raw_pointer":{"is_mutable":false,"type":{"generic":"T"}}}]],"output":{"primitive":"isize"},"is_c_variadic":false},"generics":{"params":[],"where_predicates":[{"bound_predicate":{"type":{"generic":"T"},"bounds":[{"trait_bound":{"trait":{"path":"Sized","id":7,"args":null},"generic_params":[],"modifier":"none"}}],"generic_params":[]}}]},"header":{"is_const":true,"is_unsafe":true,"is_async":false,"abi":"Rust"},"has_body":true}}},"30206":{"id":30206,"crate_id":0,"name":"rmatches","span":{"filename":"library\\core\\src\\str\\mod.rs","begin":[2047,5],"end":[2052,6]},"visibility":"public","docs":"Returns an iterator over the disjoint matches of a pattern within this\nstring slice, yielded in reverse order.\n\nThe [pattern] can be a `&str`, [`char`], a slice of [`char`]s, or a\nfunction or closure that determines if a character matches.\n\n[`char`]: prim@char\n[pattern]: self::pattern\n\n# Iterator behavior\n\nThe returned iterator requires that the pattern supports a reverse\nsearch, and it will be a [`DoubleEndedIterator`] if a forward/reverse\nsearch yields the same elements.\n\nFor iterating from the front, the [`matches`] method can be used.\n\n[`matches`]: str::matches\n\n# Examples\n\n```\nlet v: Vec<&str> = \"abcXXXabcYYYabc\".rmatches(\"abc\").collect();\nassert_eq!(v, [\"abc\", \"abc\", \"abc\"]);\n\nlet v: Vec<&str> = \"1abc2abc3\".rmatches(char::is_numeric).collect();\nassert_eq!(v, [\"3\", \"2\", \"1\"]);\n```","links":{"str::matches":30195,"prim@char":10663,"self::pattern":30602,"`DoubleEndedIterator`":69},"attrs":[{"other":"#[attr = Stability {stability: Stability {level: Stable {since: Version(RustcVersion {major: 1,\nminor: 2, patch: 0})}, feature: \"str_matches\"}}]"},{"other":"#[attr = Inline(Hint)]"}],"deprecation":null,"inner":{"function":{"sig":{"inputs":[["self",{"borrowed_ref":{"lifetime":null,"is_mutable":false,"type":{"generic":"Self"}}}],["pat",{"generic":"P"}]],"output":{"resolved_path":{"path":"RMatches","id":8902,"args":{"angle_bracketed":{"args":[{"lifetime":"'_"},{"type":{"generic":"P"}}],"constraints":[]}}}},"is_c_variadic":false},"generics":{"params":[{"name":"P","kind":{"type":{"bounds":[{"trait_bound":{"trait":{"path":"Pattern","id":8874,"args":null},"generic_params":[],"modifier":"none"}}],"default":null,"is_synthetic":false}}}],"where_predicates":[{"bound_predicate":{"type":{"qualified_path":{"name":"Searcher","args":{"angle_bracketed":{"args":[{"lifetime":"'a"}],"constraints":[]}},"self_type":{"generic":"P"},"trait":{"path":"","id":8874,"args":null}}},"bounds":[{"trait_bound":{"trait":{"path":"ReverseSearcher","id":25733,"args":{"angle_bracketed":{"args":[{"lifetime":"'a"}],"constraints":[]}}},"generic_params":[],"modifier":"none"}}],"generic_params":[{"name":"'a","kind":{"lifetime":{"outlives":[]}}}]}}]},"header":{"is_const":false,"is_unsafe":false,"is_async":false,"abi":"Rust"},"has_body":true}}},"12259":{"id":12259,"crate_id":0,"name":null,"span":null,"visibility":"default","docs":null,"links":{},"attrs":[],"deprecation":null,"inner":{"impl":{"is_unsafe":false,"generics":{"params":[],"where_predicates":[]},"provided_trait_methods":[],"trait":{"path":"Send","id":5,"args":null},"for":{"resolved_path":{"path":"uint8x16x2_t","id":9101,"args":null}},"items":[],"is_negative":false,"is_synthetic":true,"blanket_impl":null}}},"34057":{"id":34057,"crate_id":0,"name":null,"span":{"filename":"library\\core\\src\\convert\\mod.rs","begin":[827,1],"end":[837,2]},"visibility":"default","docs":null,"links":{},"attrs":[],"deprecation":null,"inner":{"impl":{"is_unsafe":false,"generics":{"params":[{"name":"T","kind":{"type":{"bounds":[],"default":null,"is_synthetic":false}}},{"name":"U","kind":{"type":{"bounds":[],"default":null,"is_synthetic":false}}}],"where_predicates":[{"bound_predicate":{"type":{"generic":"U"},"bounds":[{"trait_bound":{"trait":{"path":"Into","id":63,"args":{"angle_bracketed":{"args":[{"type":{"generic":"T"}}],"constraints":[]}}},"generic_params":[],"modifier":"none"}}],"generic_params":[]}}]},"provided_trait_methods":[],"trait":{"path":"TryFrom","id":163,"args":{"angle_bracketed":{"args":[{"type":{"generic":"U"}}],"constraints":[]}}},"for":{"resolved_path":{"path":"uint8x16x2_t","id":9101,"args":null}},"items":[700,702],"is_negative":false,"is_synthetic":false,"blanket_impl":{"generic":"T"}}}},"16110":{"id":16110,"crate_id":0,"name":null,"span":{"filename":"library\\core\\src\\..\\..\\portable-simd\\crates\\core_simd\\src\\ops.rs","begin":[189,1],"end":[246,2]},"visibility":"default","docs":null,"links":{},"attrs":[],"deprecation":null,"inner":{"impl":{"is_unsafe":false,"generics":{"params":[{"name":"N","kind":{"const":{"type":{"primitive":"usize"},"default":null}}}],"where_predicates":[{"bound_predicate":{"type":{"primitive":"i8"},"bounds":[{"trait_bound":{"trait":{"path":"SimdElement","id":9498,"args":null},"generic_params":[],"modifier":"none"}}],"generic_params":[]}},{"bound_predicate":{"type":{"resolved_path":{"path":"LaneCount","id":9493,"args":{"angle_bracketed":{"args":[{"const":{"expr":"N","value":null,"is_literal":false}}],"constraints":[]}}}},"bounds":[{"trait_bound":{"trait":{"path":"SupportedLaneCount","id":9494,"args":null},"generic_params":[],"modifier":"none"}}],"generic_params":[]}}]},"provided_trait_methods":[],"trait":{"path":"BitAnd","id":2407,"args":null},"for":{"resolved_path":{"path":"Simd","id":9499,"args":{"angle_bracketed":{"args":[{"type":{"primitive":"i8"}},{"const":{"expr":"N","value":null,"is_literal":false}}],"constraints":[]}}}},"items":[16108,16109],"is_negative":false,"is_synthetic":false,"blanket_impl":null}}},"37908":{"id":37908,"crate_id":0,"name":"_mm_mask_cmplt_epu32_mask","span":{"filename":"library\\core\\src\\..\\..\\stdarch\\crates\\core_arch\\src\\x86\\avx512f.rs","begin":[30937,1],"end":[30939,2]},"visibility":"public","docs":"Compare packed unsigned 32-bit integers in a and b for less-than, and store the results in mask vector k using zeromask k1 (elements are zeroed out when the corresponding mask bit is not set).\n\n[Intel's documentation](https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/docs/intrinsics-guide/index.html#text=_mm_mask_cmplt_epu32_mask&expand=1053)","links":{},"attrs":[{"other":"#[<cfg_attr_trace>(test, assert_instr(vpcmp))]"},{"other":"#[attr = Stability {stability: Stability {level: Stable {since: Version(RustcVersion {major: 1,\nminor: 89, patch: 0})}, feature: \"stdarch_x86_avx512\"}}]"},{"target_feature":{"enable":["avx512f","avx512vl"]}},{"other":"#[attr = Inline(Hint)]"}],"deprecation":null,"inner":{"function":{"sig":{"inputs":[["k1",{"resolved_path":{"path":"__mmask8","id":35946,"args":null}}],["a",{"resolved_path":{"path":"__m128i","id":9308,"args":null}}],["b",{"resolved_path":{"path":"__m128i","id":9308,"args":null}}]],"output":{"resolved_path":{"path":"__mmask8","id":35946,"args":null}},"is_c_variadic":false},"generics":{"params":[],"where_predicates":[]},"header":{"is_const":false,"is_unsafe":false,"is_async":false,"abi":"Rust"},"has_body":true}}},"19961":{"id":19961,"crate_id":0,"name":"shr","span":{"filename":"library\\core\\src\\ops\\bit.rs","begin":[639,1],"end":[639,70]},"visibility":"default","docs":null,"links":{},"attrs":[{"other":"#[attr = Inline(Hint)]"},{"other":"#[attr = TrackCaller]"}],"deprecation":null,"inner":{"function":{"sig":{"inputs":[["self",{"generic":"Self"}],["other",{"borrowed_ref":{"lifetime":null,"is_mutable":false,"type":{"primitive":"u128"}}}]],"output":{"qualified_path":{"name":"Output","args":null,"self_type":{"primitive":"i128"},"trait":{"path":"Shr","id":4656,"args":{"angle_bracketed":{"args":[{"type":{"primitive":"u128"}}],"constraints":[]}}}}},"is_c_variadic":false},"generics":{"params":[],"where_predicates":[]},"header":{"is_const":false,"is_unsafe":false,"is_async":false,"abi":"Rust"},"has_body":true}}},"41759":{"id":41759,"crate_id":0,"name":"cmpxchg16b","span":{"filename":"library\\core\\src\\..\\..\\stdarch\\crates\\core_arch\\src\\x86_64\\cmpxchg16b.rs","begin":[44,1],"end":[55,2]},"visibility":"public","docs":"Compares and exchange 16 bytes (128 bits) of data atomically.\n\nThis intrinsic corresponds to the `cmpxchg16b` instruction on `x86_64`\nprocessors. It performs an atomic compare-and-swap, updating the `ptr`\nmemory location to `val` if the current value in memory equals `old`.\n\n# Return value\n\nThis function returns the previous value at the memory location. If it is\nequal to `old` then the memory was updated to `new`.\n\n# Memory Orderings\n\nThis atomic operation has the same semantics of memory orderings as\n`AtomicUsize::compare_exchange` does, only operating on 16 bytes of memory\ninstead of just a pointer.\n\nThe failure ordering must be [`Ordering::SeqCst`], [`Ordering::Acquire`] or\n[`Ordering::Relaxed`].\n\nFor more information on memory orderings here see the `compare_exchange`\ndocumentation for other `Atomic*` types in the standard library.\n\n# Unsafety\n\nThis method is unsafe because it takes a raw pointer and will attempt to\nread and possibly write the memory at the pointer. The pointer must also be\naligned on a 16-byte boundary.\n\nThis method also requires the `cmpxchg16b` CPU feature to be available at\nruntime to work correctly. If the CPU running the binary does not actually\nsupport `cmpxchg16b` and the program enters an execution path that\neventually would reach this function the behavior is undefined.","links":{"`Ordering::Relaxed`":27625,"`Ordering::SeqCst`":27623,"`Ordering::Acquire`":27624},"attrs":[{"other":"#[<cfg_attr_trace>(miri, track_caller)]"},{"other":"#[<cfg_attr_trace>(test,\nassert_instr(cmpxchg16b, success = Ordering::SeqCst, failure =\nOrdering::SeqCst))]"},{"other":"#[attr = Stability {stability: Stability {level: Stable {since: Version(RustcVersion {major: 1,\nminor: 67, patch: 0})}, feature: \"cmpxchg16b_intrinsic\"}}]"},{"target_feature":{"enable":["cmpxchg16b"]}},{"other":"#[attr = Inline(Hint)]"}],"deprecation":null,"inner":{"function":{"sig":{"inputs":[["dst",{"raw_pointer":{"is_mutable":true,"type":{"primitive":"u128"}}}],["old",{"primitive":"u128"}],["new",{"primitive":"u128"}],["success",{"resolved_path":{"path":"crate::sync::atomic::Ordering","id":8789,"args":null}}],["failure",{"resolved_path":{"path":"crate::sync::atomic::Ordering","id":8789,"args":null}}]],"output":{"primitive":"u128"},"is_c_variadic":false},"generics":{"params":[],"where_predicates":[]},"header":{"is_const":false,"is_unsafe":true,"is_async":false,"abi":"Rust"},"has_body":true}}},"2014":{"id":2014,"crate_id":0,"name":"count_zeros","span":{"filename":"library\\core\\src\\num\\saturating.rs","begin":[891,1],"end":[891,77]},"visibility":"public","docs":"Returns the number of zeros in the binary representation of `self`.\n\n# Examples\n\n```\nuse std::num::Saturating;\n\nassert_eq!(Saturating(!0u32).count_zeros(), 0);\n```","